# =============================================================================
#  SEM -> Abaqus grinding project
#
#  The working tree is ~4.2 GB, almost all of it REGENERABLE Abaqus decks and
#  scratch output. Fourteen files are over 100 MB, which GitHub rejects outright
#  (612 MB x2 in ARC30_CUTS, nine ~170 MB grinding_actual_33*.inp, 188 MB in
#  PRESENT, 148 MB STL, 100 MB in WP200). So this file is not tidiness -- without
#  it the push simply fails.
#
#  WHAT IS TRACKED, deliberately:
#    semgrit/, semgrit_multi/     the pipeline
#    *.for                        the subroutines: the actual scientific content
#    verify_*.py, _make_*.py      the gates and the builders
#    *.ipynb                      both Colab notebooks
#    WHEEL_FIXED/                 the measured grain library -- everything
#                                 downstream is derived from it
#    FINAL_RIGID/, SINGLE_GRIT/   the frozen reference decks _check_presets.py
#                                 compares against. Cannot be regenerated by
#                                 definition, which is the whole point of them.
#    RUN_ME/, RUN_ME_SIC/         the submittable packages (the deliverable),
#                                 EXCEPT the .inp themselves -- see the note
#                                 further down; they are now 88-155 MB each and
#                                 are rebuilt by _make_run_packages.py --all
#    PROBE_*/                     the field-variable probes
#    error/                       the .dat files from the one real submission
#    *.tif                        the SEM micrographs -- source data
#    CAE_SCRIPTS/                 hand-written CAE repair utilities. Nothing
#                                 in the pipeline emits them, so unlike every
#                                 other *_import_into_cae.py in the tree they
#                                 cannot be regenerated.
#    REPOST/                      re-post-processing kit for the six .odb
#                                 files that have already been solved
#    obd results/                 the force and energy traces those six runs
#                                 produced. The .odb files themselves are
#                                 excluded (GB each); these are the numbers.
#
#  Anything regenerable by running a script is NOT tracked. Rebuild with:
#    python _make_run_packages.py --all
#    python _make_probe.py sandstone && python _make_probe.py silicon_carbide
#    python _make_notebook.py && python _make_notebook2.py
# =============================================================================

# ---- oversized generated decks (each >100 MB: GitHub hard-rejects these) -----
grinding_actual_33*.inp
restart33.inp

# The RUN_ME* decks themselves. These USED to be ~10 MB and were tracked, which
# is what the note above describes. Refining the mesh to put five elements across
# each material's own critical depth of cut -- and dropping the axial element from
# 1.5385 um to 0.15 um to fix a 51:1 aspect ratio -- took the workpiece from 54,080
# to 612,480 (sandstone) and 872,320 (SiC) elements, and the .inp files with it:
#
#     single abrasive     8.1 MB  ->   88 MB / 124 MB
#     multi / energy      9.8 MB  ->  110 MB / 155 MB
#
# Eleven of them are now over GitHub's 100 MB hard limit, so the push fails with
# them in. They are also the most regenerable thing in the tree -- the build is
# deterministic, seeded, and refuses to ship a package that does not verify:
#
#     python _make_run_packages.py --all
#
# Everything AROUND them is still tracked, deliberately: run.bat/run.sh, the
# subroutines, the report JSONs, the placements CSVs, the post-processing
# scripts, MANIFEST.json and README.md. So the deliverable's shape, its
# provenance and its commands stay in git and reviewable; only the multi-hundred-
# megabyte node tables that any checkout can rebuild are left out.
RUN_ME/**/*.inp
RUN_ME_SIC/**/*.inp
RUN_ME/**/*.npy
RUN_ME_SIC/**/*.npy
RUN_ME/grain_library.pkl
RUN_ME_SIC/grain_library.pkl

# RUN_TRAJECTORY/ is the same deal: the three scallop-trajectory decks are 3.7,
# 25 and 33 MB. Rebuild all three with
#     python _make_trajectory_jobs.py
RUN_TRAJECTORY/**/*.inp
ARC30_CUTS/
PRESENT/
PRESENT_ARC30/
PRESENT_FINAL/
PRESENT_FULLWHEEL/
WHEEL_30DEG_OUTPUT/
WP200/

# ---- other generated deck / output trees -------------------------------------
D50_MODEL/
FAST_MODEL/
FINAL_MODEL/
SIM_READY/
SIM_STONE/
WHEEL_ONLY/
MAIL_WHEEL_ONLY/
MAIL_TO_WORK_PC/
DIAMOND_11_RESULTS/
results/
wheel_30deg/
cad/
CAE_CHECK/
# NOT CAE_SCRIPTS/ -- see the force-add at the bottom. Its three files are
# hand-written CAE repair utilities, not pipeline output.

# ---- scratch directories written by the gates and builders -------------------
# _colabcheck holds the notebook payload unpacked; _runbuild_* is where the
# packages are assembled before being copied into RUN_ME*.
_colabcheck/
_envchk/
_hybridchk/
_presetchk/
_pipeA/
_pipeB/
_runbuild*/
_cadv/
_view/
_eq/
_eqglb/
_hybrid_test/*.exe
_hybrid_test/*.o
_hybrid_test/*.mod

# ---- reference papers --------------------------------------------------------
# 94 MB of third-party PDFs. Excluded for size and because redistributing
# published papers from a public repo is not ours to do. The findings that
# matter are quoted, with page numbers, in the subroutine headers and the
# project memory notes.
papers/

